derivations.claim:
Because $H$ is complex symmetric and invertible, some vector $v$ has $v^THv
e0$: otherwise polarization would give $u^THv=0$ for every $u,v$, contradicting invertibility. Extend $v$ to a basis and use this as a complex linear coordinate change, so $H_{11}
e0$. From now on, compose each coordinate change with the preceding ones; $\Psi$ denotes the final map from the original coordinates. The holomorphic equation $\partial_{z_1}\phi(z_1,z')=0$ has a unique holomorphic solution $z_1=\psi(z')$ near $0$ by [F3] and [F4], since $\partial_{z_1}\phi(0)=0$ and $\partial_{z_1}^2\phi(0)=H_{11}
e0$.
